name: the editor
boss: Mux
flavor: Detach all you like. I keep the session.
brief: vi is on every unix that has ever existed and it is on this one. You do
brief: not have to like it; you have to be able to leave it, and to fix a
brief: config file on a machine that has nothing else.
brief:
brief: Two modes. You start in normal mode, where keys are commands. i enters
brief: insert mode, where keys are text. Escape returns. Everything else is
brief: detail you can pick up later.
brief:
brief: The one you must know: press Escape, then type :q! and Enter. That
brief: leaves without saving, from any state you have got yourself into.
---
q: open @txt@ in the editor
a: vi @txt@
ref: vi
teach: vi
---
q: open @txt@ read-only, so you cannot damage it by accident
a: vi -R @txt@
ref: vi
teach: vi -R
---
q: leave vi without saving, from normal mode
a: :q!
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: escape first, then colon
---
q: save and leave vi
a: :wq
a: :x
ref: vi
teach: vi
---
q: save without leaving
a: :w
ref: vi
teach: vi
---
q: jump to line 20 inside vi
a: :20
ref: vi
teach: vi
---
q: search forward for @term@ inside vi
a: /@term@
ref: vi
teach: vi
---
q: replace every occurrence of @old@ with @new@ in the whole file, in vi
a: :%s/@old@/@new@/g
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: the same s/// you already know from sed
---
q: delete the current line in vi
a: dd
ref: vi
teach: vi
---
q: undo the last change in vi
a: u
ref: vi
teach: vi
---
q: page through @log@ with the pager rather than the editor
a: less @log@
ref: less
teach: less
---
q: inside less, search for @term@
a: /@term@
ref: less
teach: less
---
q: inside less, jump to the end of the file
a: G
ref: less
teach: less
---
q: follow a growing file from inside less, the way tail -f does
a: F
ref: less
teach: less
---
setup: The shell itself has a vi mode. Turn it on and the command line you type
setup: at the prompt uses the same keys you just learned in the editor: Escape
setup: for normal mode, then h j k l, w, dd, and / to search your history.
q: %turn% on vi keybindings at the shell prompt
a: set -o vi
ref: set
teach: set -o
